WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:22.809
Thank you everyone for coming out.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:23.709
I'm Nick Gillespie.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.539
And this is the Reason

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.729
Speakeasy, which is a

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.919
monthly unscripted conversation

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.839
with outspoken defenders

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.699
of free thinking and heterodoxy in

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.169
an age of cancel culture and

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:36.609
groupthink.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:37.599
But we couldn't get them.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:38.441
So we have George Dawes Green.

00:00:38.442 --> 00:00:39.442
Tonight's

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:44.019
guest is George Dawes Green, whose

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.419
new book is chilling, historically

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.279
charged murder mystery

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.129
called "Kingdoms of Savannah."

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.409
And it's a fantastic read.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:55.299
I recommend everybody

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.609
get a copy and read it.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.199
George is a singular voice in

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.029
American letters, I think it's

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:03.669
fair to say, and a unique raconteur

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:05.169
that we're going to hear from him.

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:06.789
You know, we're going to see that

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:09.099
whose own past includes stints

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.049
living in a cemetery and running

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:12.939
a company that sold clothes

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:14.799
made from rare hand-woven

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.119
fabrics from Guatemala.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:17.649
So we'll talk a little bit about

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:19.899
your entrepreneurial past

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.369
as well as your kind of cultural

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:22.539
entrepreneurship.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.129
Two of his previous novels, "The

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.049
Cave Man's Valentine and the Juror,"

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:27.909
were turned into movies, one

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:30.429
of them a very bad movie by his

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:31.459
his own reckoning.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:32.679
So we'll talk a little bit about

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.839
that. And a third novel, Raven's

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.129
Drew rave reviews when it was

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.259
published in 2010.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:39.969
He's also the founder of The Moth,

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:41.949
which is celebrating its 25th

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:44.109
anniversary right this year to

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:46.150
its quarter life crisis.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.179
It sounds like people know The Moth.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:51.159
It's a wildly acclaimed

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.229
and popular event show

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:54.609
where people tell stories and a

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.259
podcast that's really redefined

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.419
personal storytelling in the digital

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.009
age. And I want to talk with you

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:01.869
about that because it's really kind

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:04.629
of fascinating to use

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.459
modern technology to go back to a

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.319
kind of very old form of

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.569
storytelling in entertainment.

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:12.459
We're going to talk about George's

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.409
experiences on the frontier

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.719
of creative expression and the ways

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.099
particularly in which the past

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.619
doggedly informs the present,

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.979
whether in his native Georgia

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.019
or post-COVID, New York

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.999
City. So, George, thank you so much

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.829
for speaking to us.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:31.449
Thank you, Nick, for having me.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.129
All right. So let's start with the

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:35.949
Kingdoms of Savannah,

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:37.179
you know, in a way.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.129
And it's it's you know, it's

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:41.139
hard. Like, I don't want to give

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:43.149
away too much of the plot because

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.039
it's a murder mystery and it's

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:46.899
a thriller and whatnot.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.729
But I guess it's safe to say that in

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.649
many ways the main character is

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.329
the city of Savannah.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.609
Can you you are not from Savannah.

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:55.929
That's not your hometown.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.099
But you're familiar with Savannah.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:59.529
But why is Savannah at the center

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:00.779
of of your novel?

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:01.599
Well, although I was.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.039
I'm not from Savannah.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.919
We we, um.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.049
Bounced around Yankee

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.299
cities for years.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:12.589
When I was a little boy.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:14.719
My dad was editor

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.759
of weekly newspapers that kept going

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:18.379
belly up one after another.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.449
And then finally, my mother,

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:23.779
who was George and insisted

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:25.159
that we go back to Georgia.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:26.869
And so we settled in Brunswick,

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:27.902
which was about an hour to

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:31.210
the south of Savannah.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.149
And so when I was about 11 or

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.609
12, as when mom started

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:38.559
to take me on,

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.689
drives up to what she called

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:41.849
our capital city.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:43.929
Because we are eighth

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:45.309
generation Savannahan.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:47.169
So we'd get

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:49.419
into her big

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.699
barge like Chevrolet

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:52.700
Bel Air,

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:55.689
and she'd always have some fancy

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:56.739
hat.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:58.569
And then we sail

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:01.869
up Route 17

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:04.209
and spend the afternoons

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.639
at cousin's houses in Savannah

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.219
in these terribly stuffy

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:10.929
parlors.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:12.789
And and then

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:15.039
I would become inculcated

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.499
with the traditions

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.629
of Savannah and the South,

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:21.609
then the stories that were

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:24.249
sort of hammered into my head.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.649
And the other thing that mother

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:30.159
loved were seances.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:31.929
And all of the cousins loved

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:32.769
seances.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:35.169
My mother's name was Inez

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:36.850
and the cousins.

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:40.349
I there were about four cousins

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:42.179
who were also named Inez, there was

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.069
just this sort of gaggle of

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:45.929
of first cousins and

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:47.129
second cousins.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.409
And they would sit around

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.329
in these parlors

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.339
and commune

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:54.340
with the dead.

